{"type": "thinking", "text": "We need solve 4 problems. Need write solutions. Let's analyze each.\n\n1. ABC387 F Count Arrays. Known solution: functional graph A (each i -> A_i). Constraint x_i <= x_{A_i}. For each connected component (functional graph with one cycle, trees feeding cycle), count assignments. Condition along edges. For a rooted tree with edges toward parent (i->A_i), values nondecreasing upward. For cycle, x_i <= x_{next} around cycle implies all equal on cycle. Then tree nodes must <= parent etc. Count per component as sum over root value t of (# assignments to tree with max constraints). Known DP: For each node, f_i(t) = number assignments in subtree with x_i = t? Actually edges point i -> parent A_i. Constraint x_i <= x_parent. For a node v with children c (A_c=v), given x_v=t, each child subtree count is F_c(t)=sum_{s<=t} f_c(s) where f_c(s) assignments child root value s and descendants. Then f_v(t)=prod_{c} F_c(t) if v not cycle. For cycle all same t: count per t = product over cycle nodes of prod child F_c(t). Then total sum_t product = ... Answer product over components of sum_{t=1}^M g_comp(t).\n\nN,M <=2025, can do O(N M) perhaps with per-component. Need handle functional graph. Compute DP over trees, using O(N*M) total? Each edge contributes operations over t. We can compute F_v(t) prefix sums of f_v. For node v, f_v(t) = product over children F_c(t). Since children processed. Total multiplication per child per t = O(NM). Then for cycle combos: g(t)=prod over cycle nodes f-ish? Actually for cycle node v with tree children, define h_v(t)=prod_{c child non-cycle} F_c(t). g(t)=prod_{v in cycle} h_v(t). Sum over t. Per component multiply h over cycle nodes for each t: O(cycle length*M), total O(NM). Fine ~4M operations.\n\nNeed detect cycles. Functional graph N nodes outdegree 1. Can use Kahn to find cycle nodes, then process tree nodes reverse topological order. Compute f for all nodes? To compute for cycle nodes, their noncycle children processed. We can compute f_v for all noncycle nodes via topological order. Then h_v for cycle.\n\nSimpler: use indegree Kahn; order of removed nodes. For each noncycle node in removal order (leaves first), compute f_v(t). But f_v depends on children. Removal order from leaves? Kahn removes indegree-zero nodes, which are leaves (no incoming children), then updates parents. So yes order is leaf to root. Process in that order: f_v[t] = product over already processed children F_c[t]? Need accumulate product into parent as we go. Initialize f_v[t]=1 for all v? For each node removed, its f_v = prod of F_children[t] where children already removed. Since children processed before v. Could maintain cur[v][t] initially 1, and when child c finalizes, multiply F_c[t] into cur[parent]. Then when v removed, f_v[t]=cur[v][t]. Good memory N*M ~4M ints ~ Python huge (~112MB+) maybe okay but high. Could avoid storing all f, only F for children? Need F_c computed; once multiplied into parent, can discard child. For cycle nodes, need h_v = cur[v] after all tree children. Then cycle g = product over h_v. We only need cur arrays for nodes not yet removed, potentially N arrays ~4M Python ints (~112 MB plus list overhead 4M*8=32MB pointers + int objects shared? Values are int objects unique maybe 28 bytes = 112MB, total ~150-200MB). Might be tight but maybe AtCoder limit unknown likely 1024MB. But can optimize with arrays? Or process components recursively DFS and merge? Another known approach O(N M) with DP per node storing f arrays; memory O(NM) maybe accepted. N,M 2025 => 4.1M, Python list of lists ints can be ~150MB. AtCoder memory often 1024 MiB. Fine. But hidden tests maybe running environment memory unknown. Better optimize.\n\nAlternative tree DP that computes polynomial? Actually f_v(t) values for all t. We can compute per component with DFS postorder and arrays. For a component, total sizes anyway N. Could use array('I') values mod 998244353 fits 32-bit. But multiplications need Python ints; storing in list. We could store cur arrays only for nodes that need them. Need not store f after use. Child's F needed only to multiply parent, then discard. With Kahn process, cur for each node can be stored; every node eventually removed. Number of live arrays might be large but at worst N leaves each length M? Wait leaves cur initialized to [1]*M; creating N arrays of length M is heavy. Can instead lazily create cur when first child contribution? For leaf, f_v(t)=1, F_v(t)=t (number s<=t) actually if child subtree condition root value s <= parent value t -> F_v(t)=sum_{s=1}^t 1 = t. Multiplying this into parent for every leaf means parent cur[t] *= t for each leaf, no need store child. General noncycle node maybe has children, need compute f_v. Could process recursive with arrays.\n\nMaybe optimize by dealing with at most one? Let's think typical solution for ABC387F: O(NM) DP where dp[v][j] = ways subtree of v given value j (?) and prefix. Memory O(NM) common in C++, Python maybe acceptable with list but we can write careful.\n\nCould derive using generating functions? N,M 2025, O(NM) fine. We can implement arrays as Python lists. Let's estimate. Each node's f array length M+1; 2025 nodes * 2026 * (8 pointer + 28 int) ~147MB, plus F arrays another ~147MB if both. But we can mutate f to prefix in place when done, and store only prefix F for each child until parent processed. In Kahn, cur[v] accumulates products (f). When v removed, convert cur[v] to prefix in place (F_v) then multiply into parent cur. After multiplication, discard cur[v] (set None). So live arrays: those of nodes not finalized. Each node cur array initially maybe none until contribution. If we lazily initialize parent when first child: parent cur starts as copy of child F. For a parent with many children, multiply. Each node's cur exists only after at least one child processed; leaves never create own cur; their F can be computed on the fly as t (list [0..M]) and multiplied to parent then discarded. But a leaf with parent maybe many leaves: first child F t, parent cur = [0,1,2..M] (integers), subsequent multiply by t. So explicit cur arrays for internal nodes only. Worst-case long chain: at any time? Kahn leaf order: chain of length N (functional graph must have cycle; consider path of length ~N with cycle at end). Process leaf, multiply its F into parent; then parent removed immediately next? In Kahn, after leaf removed, parent indegree becomes 0, queued and eventually removed; all nodes in path quickly. Live cur maybe one at a time. But could have star: one root with N-1 leaves. First leaf creates root cur; rest multiply. One array. Could have many roots waiting with contributions: structure where many parents each have leaf child, and those parents form cycle? Cycle nodes accumulate. Number of cycle nodes * M maybe large (N*M) but then one component cycle large, we need all h_v arrays simultaneously to multiply into g? Could multiply g as nodes finalized? Cycle nodes not removed by Kahn; all remain. We could process cycle nodes and combine. Worst cycle N nodes each with no tree children: each h_v(t)=1 (initial cur all ones) no need arrays. But to find g = product over cycle h_v; for cycles with tree children, h_v arrays needed. Consider cycle of length N/2, each node with one leaf child: each h_v(t)=t, and g=t^(N/2) per t. Could represent? General h arrays. Number cycle nodes*M ~N*M worst if cycle length ~N and each has tree child? But total nodes N, cycle length ~N/2 and each has one leaf => N/2 arrays of length M ~2M ints ~70MB. Still possibly okay. But we can avoid storing h arrays by aggregate g initially [1]*(M+1), and for each cycle node after computing h multiply into g, then discard h. We need determine cycle orientation and process tree children. In Kahn, tree children contribute to cycle parent cur. After Kahn, cur[v] for cycle nodes = h_v (product over noncycle children F). Then can multiply all into g, discard each. So live max for cycle nodes while Kahn processing tree children: potentially many cur arrays. Above cycle+leaves: leaf processed create cur for its cycle parent, N/2 arrays. bad but maybe still ~70MB. Could process differently to avoid per-cycle arrays by accumulate directly? When child is leaf, its F=t. Could store representation? But generally.\n\nCould use arrays of ints via Python list and hope memory. AtCoder ABC387 F constraints N,M <=2025. Typical Python solutions use O(NM) lists maybe PyPy. Our environment CPython. Need robust hidden tests. Maybe use numpy unavailable. Use `array('I')` for storage to reduce memory dramatically; operations loop Python reads/writes. But converting F multiplication loops, array access slower maybe okay ~4M operations (actually edge contributions O(NM)=4M, each modulo). That's fine <10s. Memory arrays 4 bytes each. Use array module standard lib. But initial arrays of [1]*M need. Could implement with lists for speed but risk memory.\n\nAlternative use DFS with per-node DP arrays and free after parent computed; for cycle aggregate. We can devise efficient memory O(N+M)? We process functional graph as rooted forest into cycle. For a node, combine children one by one: For a child c, recursively compute F_c array, multiply into local cur, discard. Along a deep chain recursion could hold many local cur but each maybe not initialized until returning; depth recursion >1000 maybe set recursionlimit. But memory of recursion plus arrays one per branch. For tree, postorder: compute child F, multiply into parent's accumulating array, free child. Then after all children, parent f = cur; convert to prefix F and return. This uses memory O(depth*M?) Actually parent cur arrays created as children return; if deep chain, each parent waits with one child's F? Let's see recursion: parent calls child; child returns F array to parent, parent cur = child F (or multiply into new). Then parent has array; returns to its parent. So along a chain only one array at a time because child's array becomes parent's array. For a branching tree, when processing second child, parent holds cur; child computes its own arrays and returns; then merged and freed. Peak O(depth * M) in worst? If parent has many children, at any moment parent cur + current child subtree arrays. Along recursion, each ancestor may hold a cur array while processing later children? Example root processes child1 (returns, root cur), then child2 recursion; root cur retained, child2 processes... depth chain of \"last child\" with each ancestor retaining cur => O(depth*M) arrays. Could happen. But memory maybe okay. Kahn lazy similar.\n\nMaybe there's a more clever O(NM) algorithm per component that only uses one global array? For functional graph trees, DP polynomial can be computed via merging. But not necessary.\n\nCould exploit N,M 2025 and use list-of-arrays. Let's focus correctness first, then performance.\n\n2. ABC388 F Dangerous Sugoroku. N up to 1e12, M <=2e4, A,B<=20. Need reachability. Bad intervals. Need efficient compressed DP. Since A,B small, can use bitset/interval of reachable positions. We have forbidden intervals disjoint sorted. Need determine if N reachable from 1 with jumps [A,B] avoiding bad cells. Standard solution: maintain reachable positions as union of intervals? With fixed jump window and blocked intervals, reachable set can be represented via differences in blocks. N huge but M limited, can process good segments of length huge using matrix/linear recurrence, or use interval operations.\n\nObservation: movement from reachable x to any y in [x+A,x+B] good. Reachable set possibly union of intervals increasing? Starting {1}. On empty line, reachable all >=1? With min jump A: from 1 can reach [1+A,1+B], then union expands; eventually all positions >= some threshold? Actually from 1, reachable [1+A,1+B], then from those -> [1+2A,1+2B], etc. There can be gaps when A>1? Iterating intervals: [1+kA,1+kB]; for k until next start <= current end+1? Overlap if