nts after products <1e18*27000 ~2^75 >2^64, so carries. Could reduce each state after each transition? More reductions. Or choose K=128 (16 bytes) to be safe, chunks 16 bytes. Conversion cost similar.\n\nMaybe use base 10^? Not.\n\nCan we avoid reduction and use exact integer with K=448? Then no reduction per level, but memory high as discussed. Could reduce at each level with K=96; likely okay. Number states ~ how many actually reachable? s up to30, a<=s, ec<=s, p 2 => ~2*Σ s^2 ~2*9455=18910 states, but not all (ec maybe). ~15k. Each polynomial D length maybe not 435 for all; D max depends on s and a? Actually D is exponent accumulated, maybe up to C(s,2) plus? For partial s, D max ≤C(s,2)? H transition D_inc = C(b,2)+a(b-j) with j maybe 0, combined with previous D ≤? Total edges max on s vertices = C(s,2) yes. So length ~C(s,2) ≤435, average over s ~? Sum lengths over states Σ_s s^2 * C(s,2) ~2.4e7 digits. Reduction conversion 24M chunks, maybe significant ~10s. But maybe many states not all ec/p; total ~ maybe 1e7. Need benchmark.\n\nCould improve by doing reductions using modular arithmetic packed with base that avoids carry and applying modulus to whole big integer? There is no easy digit-wise mod. Could use base 2^K with K=60 and rely on coefficients <2^60? But sums exceed. Could reduce after each addition? Then digit-wise reduction per term impossible.\n\nMaybe use numpy? Not allowed third-party, but Python standard library only. Could use array loops; slower than big ints.\n\nAlternative use original polynomial DP with big ints modulo P and modular convolution via Kronecker, reducing per state. Similar reduction cost.\n\nMaybe there is an even better basis that eliminates inclusion-exclusion sum j and reduces transitions to O(1) per b, at cost of tracking something else. Let's manipulate H_{a,b}(x). It counts bipartite graphs with b-side no isolated. In binomial basis:\nH = Σ_j (-1)^j C(b,j)(1+x)^{C(b,2)+a(b-j)}.\nThis is like evaluating. We can incorporate j by extending state with \"a\"? Maybe define a transform where transition over j is convolution in b? Not necessary.\n\nCould use inclusion-exclusion differently: Number of bipartite graphs with all b nonisolated = Σ_{S⊆b} (-1)^{|S|} (1+x)^{a(b-|S|)}. Tracking j. Fine.\n\nCould maybe swap DP to count all graphs with layers allowing isolated vertices, then use inclusion-exclusion globally over vertices not reached? But that's standard connected graph count maybe. Wait perhaps we can avoid enforcing each vertex in S_i has neighbor previous by interpreting layers as a partition into independent? If we count all graphs with edges only between consecutive chosen groups without the no-isolated condition, then a vertex might actually belong to an earlier layer, causing overcount. But maybe inclusion-exclusion across all vertices could cancel and yield a simpler formula. This is exactly what the j per layer does, but perhaps can be applied globally via signed sum over a second coloring, leading to O(N^2) states without b/j? Let's see.\n\nAn ordered partition into nonempty layers plus edges only within consecutive layers. If some vertex in layer i has no neighbor in layer i-1, its true BFS layer is earlier, so it would belong to an earlier layer. Counting all such \"pseudo-layerings\" and applying Möbius inversion might allow nonempty condition relaxed. There may be a bijection where each graph with a chosen subset? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively use exponential formula for connected graphs and distance parity might be simpler via rooted graphs. But BFS layering is unique, so inclusion-exclusion local is natural.\n\nMaybe compute with DP over layer sizes and introduce a variable for \"j\" choices; tracking D only. Already good.\n\nLet's first implement other 3 problems and test. Then focus on G with performance benchmarking.\n\nLet's create solutions for F, 388F, 388G first.\n\nProblem 1 memory/time. Let's implement using Kahn + list arrays. We can choose simpler O(NM) with arrays.\n\nLet's design 387F robust and efficient.\n\nFunctional graph A (1-indexed). We need for each component sum over t=1..M of g(t). We can process using Kahn with lazy cur arrays.\n\nOption implement recursive DFS postorder and cycle detection:\n- Build reverse children list.\n- state 0 unvisited, 1 visiting, 2 done.\n- Find cycle nodes via standard functional graph. Since N up to2025 recursion depth can be 2025 > default; set recursionlimit 10000. But recursive DP on trees depth up to2025 also okay with limit.\n- Identify cycles: use Kahn indegree to find cycle nodes (nodes with indegree >0 after removing). Then for each cycle node, mark on cycle. For tree children (nodes not on cycle), compute DP.\n\nKahn processing with cur arrays:\nInitialize `cur = [None]*(N+1)`. We don't want [1]*M per node. When child v finalized, F_v(t) array. We need multiply into parent cur.\nFor each node v in removal order (leaf to root):\n  if cur[v] is None: f_v(t)=1 for all t? Wait leaf f_v(t)=1 (no children). Then F_v(t)=prefix sum = t (for t=1..M). But if cur[v] exists, it holds product over children F_c(t), i.e., f_v(t). Then convert cur[v] to prefix F_v.\n- To multiply F_v into parent p=A[v]: if cur[p] is None, set cur[p] = F_v.copy() (or same array, since F_v no longer needed) ; else multiply elementwise cur[p][t] *= F_v[t] %mod. We can reuse F_v array as cur[p] by assigning if None. If not None, multiply and discard F_v.\nBut for a node v with no children (leaf), F_v is array [0,1,2,...,M] (note t=0 maybe 0). Could create each time; many leaves => create array per leaf, multiply into parent and discard. That's O(M) per edge anyway. Could optimize leaf contribution: if parent cur is None and child leaf, set parent cur = arange (list(range(M+1))), then next leaf multiply by t. Fine. Creating arrays for each leaf cost memory transient, time N*M =4M.\n\nAfter Kahn removal order, cur[v] for cycle nodes holds h_v(t)=product over noncycle children F_c(t). (Cycle children? In functional graph, each cycle node has exactly one cycle outgoing and maybe incoming from previous cycle node, but in Kahn, cycle nodes are not removed, so cur only gets noncycle children, not cycle predecessor. Good.)\n\nFor each cycle, compute g[t] = product over v in cycle of h_v[t], where if cur[v] None then h=1. Initialize g = [1]*(M+1) maybe. For each v in cycle:\n  arr=cur[v]\n  if arr is None: continue (all ones no effect)\n  else for t in1..M: g[t]=g[t]*arr[t]%MOD\nThen component total = sum(g[1..M]) mod.\nMultiply answer by component total.\n\nNeed ensure tree DP: F_v(t)=Σ_{s=1}^t f_v(s) where f_v(s)=∏_{child c noncycle? all children of v except if v on cycle? For noncycle v, all children are noncycle because if child on cycle then A[child] on cycle and v would be on cycle too. So cur[v] from Kahn includes all children. Good. For v with no children, f_v(t)=1. Prefix F_v=t. Good.\n\nBut when converting cur[v] to prefix: if cur[v] is None -> F_v = [0,1,...,M] possibly precompute arange list once and copy? We can set cur[v]=base.copy() or use base? Since if assigned to parent and then mutated in future multiplications, need not mutate the global base. If parent None we set cur[p]=F_v, which for leaf F_v is base list; if we pass base itself and later parent multiplies in place, base gets corrupted. So either copy or create list(range(M+1)) each leaf. N<=2025, M<=2025 => 4M elements = fine. Could use `list(range(M+1))` per leaf. For node with cur not None, need prefix in place: running s=0; for t in1..M: s=(s+cur[v][t])%mod; cur[v][t]=s; cur[v][0]=0. Note f_v(t) originally for t=1..M, cur[0] maybe 0? Initialize arrays from child F have index0=0. For nodes with cur None leaf base index0=0. For parent with multiple children, first child F assigned index0=0, multiply leaves index0 stays0. Good. Prefix loop set 0.\n\nThen multiply F_v into parent p:\n if cur[p] is None: cur[p]=F_v (transfer)\n else:\n   cp=cur[p]; cf=F_v\n   for t in1..M: cp[t]=cp[t]*cf[t]%mod\n   cur[v]=None (discard)\nBut if we transfer F_v to parent, cur[v] still points same array. Could set cur[v]=None to avoid confusion? We can set after. Kahn order means v won't be used again. But cycle nodes not in order. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: When parent p later removed, its cur array may be same as child's old F; but child no longer needed. Good.\n\nKahn removal order: compute indegree counts (number of incoming children). Queue indegree0. As remove v, decrement indegree[A[v]]; if becomes0 enqueue. This order ensures when v processed all children processed? A node's indegree counts children (including maybe cycle predecessor if on cycle? For cycle nodes, each has one incoming from cycle so indegree never0). For noncycle v, all incoming are noncycle children (a cycle node cannot point to noncycle because functional cycle's outgoing stays cycle). Thus when indegree[v] becomes0, all children removed. Good.\n\nProcess queue in order. For each v, first if cur[v] None -> base else prefix. Then transfer to parent. But if parent is v itself (self-loop on cycle), v won't be in queue. Noncycle v cannot A[v]=v? self-loop is cycle, so v cycle. Fine.\n\nCycle extraction: after Kahn, nodes with indegree>0 (or not removed) are cycle nodes. We can find cycles by walking from each unvisited removed? Use `on_cycle = [indeg[i]>0]`. Then for each i with on_cycle and not visited, follow A until back, collect.\n\nComplexity: For each noncycle edge, multiply O(M) plus prefix O(M) per node not per edge. Wait each node's cur prefix done once, so O(N*M). Each edge transfers multiplies O(M). Total ~2NM. For cycle, each cycle node h (cur) is already f not prefix; we multiply h arrays into g O(M per cycle node). Good. Total ~4M operations per test, Python fine maybe 1-2s.\n\nBut careful: cur[v] for noncycle node after children multiplications is f_v(t) = product F_c(t). Then prefix. When transfer to parent, parent's cur should multiply F_v(t), which we do. Good.\n\nLet's test sample.\n\nProblem 3 sparse table. Implement.\n\nProblem 2 as above.\n\nLet's write files and a test harness to run samples.\n\nThen G.\n\nLet's implement F first."}
{"type": "tool_call", "callId": "call_00_5eIjnVCVl4NtVpKpBd3B9068", "tool": "bash", "input": {"command": "mkdir -p solutions && cat > solutions/abc387_f.py << 'PY'\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef main():\n    data = list(map(int, sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()))\n    if not data:\n        return\n    n, m = data[0], data[1]\n    a = [0] + data[2:2+n]\n    indeg = [0]*(n+1)\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        indeg[a[i]] += 1\n    q = deque(i for i in range(1, n+1) if indeg[i] == 0)\n    cur = [None]*(n+1)\n    removed = [False]*(n+1)\n    while q:\n        v = q.popleft()\n        removed[v] = True\n        arr = cur[v]\n        if arr is None:\n            f = list(range(m+1))\n        else:\n            s = 0\n            arr[0] = 0\n            for t in range(1, m+1):\n                s += arr[t]\n                if s >= MOD:\n                    s -= MOD\n                arr[t] = s\n            f = arr\n        p = a[v]\n        cp = cur[p]\n        if cp is None:\n            cur[p] = f\n        else:\n            for t in range(1, m+1):\n                cp[t] = cp[t] * f[t] % MOD\n            cur[v] = None\n        indeg[p] -= 1\n        if indeg[p] == 0:\n            q.append(p)\n    visited = [False]*(n+1)\n    ans = 1\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        if (not removed[i]) and (not visited[i]):\n            cyc = []\n            x = i\n            while not visited[x]:\n                visited[x] = True\n                cyc.append(x)\n                x = a[x]\n            g = None\n            for v in cyc:\n                arr = cur[v]\n                if arr is None:\n                    continue\n                if g is None:\n                    g = [1]*(m+1)\n                for t in range(1, m+1):\n                    g[t] = g[t] * arr[t] % MOD\n            if g is None:\n                total = m % MOD\n            else:\n                total = sum(g[1:]) % MOD\n            ans = ans * total % MOD\n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\nPY\npython3 solutions/abc387_f.py << 'EOF'\n3 3\n2 1 1\nEOF\npython3 solutions/abc387_f.py << 'EOF'\n4 9\n1 1 1 1\nEOF\npython3 solutions/abc387_f.py << 'EOF'\n10 5\n9 4 5 5 4 2 1 5 7 2\nEOF", "description": "Implement and test ABC387 F solution"}}
